---
This page sets `scroll  reverse`. Your wheel goes the other way. Down is up.

It is disorienting, which is the point — a page is allowed to refuse the
convention. `scroll  horizontal` is the other option, and it turns the article
into columns you push sideways: [[sideways]].

## the one rule

Both bail out entirely under `prefers-reduced-motion`. Hijacking someone's
scroll is precisely the class of thing that setting exists to stop, and an
experimental site that ignores accessibility is just a badly built one.

Ctrl+wheel is left alone so zoom still works.

## other knobs on this page

`paper` and `ink` invert the palette. `align  right` sets the text ragged-left.
Neither needed a stylesheet — they are header lines.
